    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d741">

                <pb facs="0882" n="866" sameAs="frus:frus1947v08/pg866"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">817.00/8–1947</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Nicaragua</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bernbaum</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extracts]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">restricted</hi>
                        <lb/> No. 1729</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Managua</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-08-19">August 19,
                        1947</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of the Embassy’s despatch
                    no. 1726 of August 15, 1947<anchor n="62" xml:id="d741-f1"/> concerning the
                    meeting of the Constituent Assembly, I have the honor to enclose a clipping of
                    the address of Dr. Victor Manuel Román to the Assembly subsequent to his
                    selection by it as President.</p>
                <p>It may be interjected here that there is some question as to whether Dr. Román is
                    a “constitutional” or “provisional” President in the technical sense of the
                    term. The Government press invariably describes Mm as a “constitutional”
                    magistrate while the opposition press, the majority, describes his office as
                    “provisional”. As a matter of fact, it is difficult to understand how a
                    constitutional president can be selected before there is a Constitution.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>As the Embassy reported telegraphically (see Embassy’s telegram no. 306 of August
                    15, 1947<anchor n="62" xml:id="d741-f2"/>), the selection of Dr. Román has
                    provoked a general reaction of respect for him as an individual but no respect
                    whatsoever for the maneuver which put him into office and which is considered as
                    nothing less than a continuation of the Somoza regime. …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . .</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Maurice</hi> M. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Bernbaum</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
